.TH CHMAP_INIT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_init_full \- create a hash map with a custom allocator, hashing procedure and key equality procedure into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_init_full(hm_name, mmgmt_procs, custom_hashing_proc,
                custom_key_equality_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a fully customized hash map and assigns it to hm_name. The caller
must declare the handle first, with chmap_declare(3) or
chmap_declare_scoped(3). The key and value types are not arguments here:
the macro reads them from the companion type variables that the declare
macro created.

This macro is the widest of the init macros: chmap_init(3) is this macro
with the default allocator and the map's own hashing, chmap_init_mp(3)
gives only an allocator, and chmap_init_ch(3) gives only a hashing
procedure. Each of the three arguments can be NULL, which selects the
matching default, so this one macro also covers a call site that chooses
them at run time.

The one-step equivalents are chmap_construct_full(3) and
chmap_construct_full_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              The already declared map handle to create the map into
mmgmt_procs          Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                     default allocator
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for the declared key type
custom_key_equality_proc
                     A ccol_key_equality_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's
                     own key comparison
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure receives a pointer to the key bytes and their length,
and returns an unsigned long. It must return the same value for two keys
that the map considers equal; for a char * key, equal means equal
contents, not an equal pointer.

The return value does not need to avalanche, because the map finalizes
the value that a custom procedure returns with its own bit-mixing step
before it reduces the value to a slot or a bucket. A procedure that puts
all of its entropy in the low bits therefore still spreads across the
table.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
custom_key_equality_proc receives both keys and both sizes, and returns
true when the two are the same key. The map uses it in place of its
built-in comparison. It must agree with custom_hashing_proc: two keys
that it reports as equal must get the same hash. A key equality procedure
without a hashing procedure makes the creation fail, and this macro then
stops the program. A struct key with padding bytes needs both
procedures; see chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails; call
chmap_create_full(3) directly when the caller must handle a failure
instead of the failure being fatal
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets; use
chmap_create_full(3) to ask for a different initial bucket count
The macro copies the memory management procedures into the map, so the
caller's own ccol_memmgmt_procs_t need not outlive the call. Those
procedures, the hashing procedure and the key equality procedure must
stay callable for the whole life of the map
A custom hashing procedure does not change which implementation strategy
the map picks from the declared key and value types, but a custom key
equality procedure selects separate chaining
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
unsigned long hash_bytes(const void *p, size_t size);

chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
chmap_init_full(scores, &procs, hash_bytes, NULL);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_init_ch (3),
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_construct_full_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
